From: PortalRex <35444194+PortalRex@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 08:01:47 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] control: count the saves and restores done and the
 requests failed; export ChocoGetSnapshotStatus so a plugin can wait for the
 operation it asked for

---
 AGENTS.md       |  2 ++
 src/control.cpp | 10 ++++++++--
 src/control.h   |  6 ++++++
 src/plugin.cpp  | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++
 4 files changed, 36 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/AGENTS.md b/AGENTS.md
index cf6c413..d643cdc 100644
--- a/AGENTS.md
+++ b/AGENTS.md
@@ -303,6 +303,8 @@ the memory it brought back is the locked state of that moment. `snap_opt render_
 behaviour (a snapshot taken that way can only be restored that way). Tested with -vulkan: 60-80 restores
 in a row in sp_a2_laser_stairs and mp_coop_laser_2 (the maps that crashed) and level changes after them.
 
+**Exports for plugins that drive snapshots** (`src/plugin.cpp`): `ChocoRestoresDeviceState()` (1 when a restore sets the Direct3D device back too) and `ChocoGetSnapshotStatus(ChocoSnapshotStatus*)`: counts of saves done, restores done and requests failed (`control::Done()`; every request ends up in exactly one of them, also one refused at the command or dropped by a level change), and whether queued rendering stays on. A plugin compares the counts with what they were when it sent `snap_save` / `snap_load`, instead of waiting a number of frames.
+
 Next ideas:
 - Shorter snap_load latency: a request waits 4 frames for the workers to drain unless the threading
   cvars are already forced - now longer than the restore itself (~9 ms). Keeping them forced while a
